 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, not yet.** Inflation is above the Reserve Bank's 2–3% target band, and the most recent figures show it has been rising since mid-2025.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026, up from 2.4% in April 2025. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and dipped to 3.5% in July before rising again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 3.2% in April 2025. It has been above 3% since mid-2025 and has risen steadily since April 2026. This is the better guide to underlying pressure, and it is still well above target.\n- **Electricity:** This is a big driver of the headline. Prices were 13.2% higher than a year earlier in August 2026, after falling sharply through late 2024 and early 2025. Its swings make the headline noisy.\n- **Rents:** The one clear improvement. Rent inflation has eased from 7.5% in early 2024 to 3.6% in August 2026, though it has flattened at that level since May.\n\n**The policy response:** The RBA cut the cash rate from 4.35% to 3.60% between February and December 2025. It then reversed course and raised it back to 4.35% between February and June 2026. It has held there since, with a 4.36% monthly average for September 2026. The rate rises suggest the central bank sees inflation as not yet under control.\n\nThe latest CPI data is for August 2026. The September quarter figures will be released shortly and will show whether the trend has turned.",
